Q: css opacity transition on click A: <p>In 2026 we have now the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/At-rules/@starting-style" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><strong>@starting-style</strong> CSS at-rule</a><br />
Use it to define that the initial <code>opacity</code> is <code>0</code></p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function makeImage () {
  $('#image').remove(); // Remove if already exists
  $("body").append(`&lt;img class="fadeIn" id="image" src="https://picsum.photos/id/237/180/180"&gt;`);
};

$("#makeButton").on("click", makeImage);</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.fadeIn {
  transition: opacity 3s ease 0.3s;
  @starting-style {
    opacity: 0;
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;button type="button" id="makeButton"&gt;Create and fade-in&lt;/button&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.7.1.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>And here's an example without jQuery:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const el = (sel, par = document) =&gt; par.querySelector(sel);
const newEl = (tag, prop) =&gt; Object.assign(document.createElement(tag), prop);

const makeImage = () =&gt; {
  el("#image")?.remove(); // Remove if exists
  el("body").append(newEl("img", {
    className: "fadeIn",
    id: "image",
    src: `//picsum.photos/id/237/180/180`,
  }));  
};
el("#makeButton").addEventListener("click", makeImage);</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.fadeIn {
  transition: opacity 3s ease 0.3s;
  @starting-style {
    opacity: 0;
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;button type="button" id="makeButton"&gt;Create and fade-in&lt;/button&gt;&lt;br&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: How to write automation tests for elements/processes that are frequently used elsewhere? A: <p>Both approaches are relevant — and in practice you’ll end up using <strong>all of them</strong>, because good automation isn’t about one perfect pattern, it’s about layering tests so failures tell you <em>where</em> the problem is without wasting half a day debugging.</p>
<p>When you’ve got a UI element that appears everywhere (like that calendar widget), you treat it as a shared component, and I test it in three ways:</p>
<h3><strong>1. Small, functional tests</strong></h3>
<p>Fast‑feedback tests. Hit the page, interact with the calendar, save the <em>minimum</em> amount of form data, and assert the field is populated correctly.</p>
<p>This gives you clear and early warning if the widget breaks, it will keep failures isolated — so, if the calendar dies, you won’t have 40 unrelated tests failing and hiding the real issue, making you trawl through the tests to find the common step failure.</p>
<h3><strong>2. End‑to‑end flows</strong></h3>
<p>Still important. These are your smoke tests after a deployment. They exercise the calendar <em>as part of a real user journey</em> — booking, publishing, cancelling, whatever.</p>
<p>You’d tag these as <code>@smoke</code> so they run as a quick subset after a release.</p>
<h3><strong>3. Component‑focused examples</strong></h3>
<p>This is the neat trick.</p>
<p>If the calendar widget is used across multiple forms, you create a single reusable step:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>“Then I access the entry calendar with date 24 Jan 2026”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>And you back it with a Cucumber step that knows how to map labels to HTML elements and populate the date field.</p>
<p>Then you use <strong>Examples</strong> to run the same calendar interaction across multiple pages:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>And on the form with URL &lt;page&gt; I enter the date of &lt;date&gt; to the &lt;form&gt; calendar

Examples:
| page                                         | date       | form    |
| https://automationintesting.online/draft     | 24/12/2026 | draft   |
| https://automationintesting.online/publish   | 01/10/2026 | publish |
| https://automationintesting.online/cancel    | 20/10/2026 | cancel  |
</code></pre>
<p>This gives you a <strong>quick, targeted test suite</strong> for the widget itself — perfect when a new version of the calendar is rolled out. Tag these as <code>@calendar-widget</code> so you can run them independently.</p>
<h3><strong>Your step definition stays clean</strong></h3>
<p>Something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Then(&quot;on form with URL {string} I enter the date of {string} to the {string} calendar&quot;)
public void populateForm(String url, String date, String calendarName) throws Exception {

    driver.get(url);

    CalendarWidget calendar = new CalendarWidget(driver);

    calendar.selectDate(calendarName, date);
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public class CalendarWidget {

    private final WebDriver driver;

    public CalendarWidget(WebDriver driver) {
        this.driver = driver;
    }

    public void selectDate(String calendarName, String dateString) {

        LocalDate target = LocalDate.parse(dateString, DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(&quot;dd/MM/yyyy&quot;));

        // 1. Open the calendar
        WebElement calendarInput = driver.findElement(By.id(calendarName));
        calendarInput.click();

        // 2. Navigate to correct month/year
        navigateToMonth(target);

        // 3. Select the correct day
        String daySelector = String.format(
            &quot;//div[contains(@class,'react-datepicker__day') and text()='%d']&quot;,
            target.getDayOfMonth()
        );

        driver.findElement(By.xpath(daySelector)).click();

        // 4. Assert the field is populated
        Assertions.assertEquals(dateString, calendarInput.getAttribute(&quot;value&quot;));
    }

    private void navigateToMonth(LocalDate target) {

        while (true) {
            WebElement header = driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(&quot;.react-datepicker__current-month&quot;));
            YearMonth displayed = YearMonth.parse(header.getText(), DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(&quot;MMMM yyyy&quot;));

            if (displayed.equals(YearMonth.from(target))) {
                break;
            }

            if (displayed.isBefore(YearMonth.from(target))) {
                driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(&quot;.react-datepicker__navigation--next&quot;)).click();
            } else {
                driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(&quot;.react-datepicker__navigation--previous&quot;)).click();
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>You centralise the logic once, and every test benefits from it.<br />
So don’t pick one approach — layer them:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Small functional tests</strong> → fast feedback, minimal failure blast radius (best as part of TDD/BDD)</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>End‑to‑end flows</strong> → smoke tests after deployment</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Component‑focused examples</strong> → rapid validation of shared widgets</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>This gives you stability, speed, and clarity when something breaks.</p>
<p>Hope this helps</p>

Q: PowerShell script with `-WhatIf` support A: <p>Use the <code>ShouldProcess</code> system.</p>
<pre><code>function Test-Whatif {
    # It must be called in the CmdletBinding parameters.   
    [CmdletBinding(SupportsShouldProcess)]
    param ()
    process {
        if ($PSCmdlet.ShouldProcess( &quot;Should you actuall perfomorm for real the actions intended in the script?&quot;)) {
            write-host &quot;This happens normally, and if you give confirmation when using the -Confirm parameter&quot; -ForegroundColor Green
        }else{
            Write-Host &quot;This happens if you use -WhatIf, or if you deny a confirmation when using the -Confirm parameter&quot; -ForegroundColor Cyan
        }                
    }
}

Test-Whatif
Test-Whatif -WhatIf
Test-Whatif -Confirm
</code></pre>
<p>As a simpler alternative, you can simply check <code>$WhatIfPreference</code>(or <code>$ConfirmPreference</code> for <code>-Confirm</code>)[thanks @Panagiotis-Kanavos
for the reminder]:</p>
<pre><code>function Test-Whatif {
    [CmdletBinding(SupportsShouldProcess)]
    param ()
    process {
        'IF($WhatIfPreference) returns {0}' -f $WhatIfPreference | Write-Host
    }
}

Test-Whatif
Test-Whatif -WhatIf
Test-Whatif -WhatIf:$false
Test-Whatif -WhatIf:$true
</code></pre>
<p>If what you are doing is sufficiently perilious to require a <code>-WhatIf</code> parameter, I would suggest to also implement the <code>-Confirm</code> parameter with the <code>ShouldProcess</code> system.</p>
